1
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:41,720
CONVERSATION 1
Excuse me.

2
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:44,240
Where’s your rock music section?

3
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:45,940
Rock music?

4
00:00:45,940 --> 00:00:48,140
I’m sorry, we’re a Jazz store.

5
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:50,780
We don’t have any rock and roll.

6
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:56,040
Oh, you only have Jazz music, nothing else.

7
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:56,960
That’s right.

8
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:02,100
We’re the only record store in London dedicated exclusively to Jazz.

9
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:04,980
Actually, we’re more than just a record store.

10
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:15,080
We have a cafe and library upstairs and a ticket office down the hall where you can buy tickets to all the major Jazz concerts in the city.

11
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:21,440
Also we have our own studio next door where reproduce albums for up and coming artists.

12
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:25,420
We are committed to fostering new music talent.

13
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:27,620
Wow, that’s so cool.

14
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:30,860
I guess there’s not much of a Jazz scene anymore.

15
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:32,460
Not like they used to be.

16
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:36,040
But here you’re trying to promote this great music genre.

17
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:36,600
Yes.

18
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:42,220
Indeed, nowadays most people like to listen to pop and rock music.

19
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:46,840
Hip hop music from America is also getting more and more popular.

20
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:55,840
So as a result, there are fewer listeners of Jazz, which is a great shame because it’s an incredibly rich genre.

21
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:01,700
But that’s not to say there isn’t any good new Jazz music being made out there anymore.

22
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:03,280
Far from it.

23
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:06,340
It’s just a much smaller market today.

24
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:09,920
So how would you define Jazz?

25
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:14,600
Interestingly enough, there’s no agreed upon definition of Jazz.

26
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:20,820
Indeed, there are many different styles of Jazz, some have singing, but most don’t.

27
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:23,280
Some are electric and some aren’t.

28
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:27,360
Some contain live experimentation, but not always.

29
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:30,440
While there’s no simple definition for it.

30
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:32,880
Allow, there are many different styles of Jazz.

31
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:35,780
You simply know it when you hear it.

32
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:36,500
Honestly.

33
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:40,700
The only way to know what Jazz is, listen to it yourself.

34
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:42,100
As the great trumpet player.

35
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:54,520
Louis Armstrong said, if you’ve got to ask, you’ll never know.

36
00:02:54,520 --> 00:03:11,060
Q1. What do we learn about the woman’s store?

37
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:29,560
Q2. What does the man say about Jazz music?

38
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:47,120
Q3. What does the woman say about Jazz?

39
00:03:47,120 --> 00:04:07,200
Q4. What should you do to appreciate different styles of Jazz according to the woman?

40
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:10,960
CONVERSATION 2
How did it go at the bank this morning?

41
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:12,040
Not well.

42
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:14,440
My proposal was rejected.

43
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:15,240
Really?

44
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:16,440
But why?

45
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:17,780
Bunch of reasons.

46
00:04:17,780 --> 00:04:22,160
For starters, they said my credit history was not good enough.

47
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:24,980
Did they say how you could improve that?

48
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:34,700
Yes, they said that after five more years of paying my mortgage, then I will become a more viable candidate for a business loan.

49
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:38,700
But right now it’s too risky for them to lend me money.

50
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:43,340
They fear I will default on any business loan I’m given.

51
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:45,060
That doesn’t sound fair.

52
00:04:45,060 --> 00:04:47,220
Your business idea is amazing.

53
00:04:47,220 --> 00:04:49,240
Did you show them your business plan?

54
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:50,540
What did they say?

55
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:55,900
They didn’t really articulate any position regarding the actual business plan.

56
00:04:55,900 --> 00:05:00,180
They simply looked at my credit history and determined it was not good enough.

57
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:06,380
They said the bank has strict guidelines and requirements as to who they can lend money to.

58
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:10,860
And I simply don’t meet their financial threshold.

59
00:05:10,860 --> 00:05:13,200
What if you ask for a smaller amount?

60
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:18,700
Maybe you could gather capital from other sources, smaller loans from more lenders.

61
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:20,260
You don’t get it.

62
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:26,760
It doesn’t matter the size of the loan I ask for, or the type of business I propose.

63
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:28,180
That’s all inconsequential.

64
00:05:28,180 --> 00:05:39,740
The first thing every bank will do is study how much money I have and how much debt I have before they decide whether or not to lend me any more money.

65
00:05:39,740 --> 00:05:46,320
If I want to continue ahead with this dream of only my own business, I have no other choice.

66
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:54,800
But to build up my own finances, I need around 20% more in personal savings and 50% less debt.

67
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:56,180
That’s all there is to it.

68
00:05:56,180 --> 00:05:58,180
I see now.

69
00:05:58,180 --> 00:06:03,480
Well, it’s a huge pity that they rejected your request, but don’t lose hope.

70
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:19,998
I still think that your idea is great and that you would turn it into a phenomenal success.

71
00:06:19,998 --> 00:06:34,680
Q5. What did the woman do this morning?

72
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:52,420
Q6. Why was the woman’s proposal rejected?

73
00:06:52,420 --> 00:07:10,140
Q7. What is the woman planning to do?

74
00:07:10,140 --> 00:07:58,070
Q8. What does the man suggest the woman do?

75
00:07:58,070 --> 00:08:02,150
PASSAGE 1
There’s a lot about Leo Sanchez and his farm in Salinas, California.

76
00:08:02,150 --> 00:08:04,210
That seems unusual.

77
00:08:04,210 --> 00:08:12,870
The national average farm size is around 440 acres, but his is only one acre.

78
00:08:12,870 --> 00:08:20,310
The average age of farmers hovers around 58 years old, but he is just 26.

79
00:08:20,310 --> 00:08:30,530
And Sanchez constantly attempts to improve everything from seeding techniques out in the field to the promotion and sale of his produce online.

80
00:08:30,530 --> 00:08:36,190
This is evidence of an experimental approach.

81
00:08:36,190 --> 00:08:45,870
It’s an approach not dictated by the confines of conventional large scale agriculture lead by international corporations.

82
00:08:45,870 --> 00:09:01,220
While farming is often difficult for both the body and mind, Sanchez says he and many of his fellow young farmers are motivated by desire to set a new standard for agriculture.

83
00:09:01,220 --> 00:09:09,180
Many of them are employing a multitude of technologies, some new and some not so new.

84
00:09:09,180 --> 00:09:16,600
Recently, Sanchez bought a hand operated tool which pulls out weeds and loosen soil.

85
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:21,180
It actually dates back to at least 1701.

86
00:09:21,180 --> 00:09:31,000
It stands in sharp contrast to Sanchez’s other gadget, a gas powered flame rekiller, invented in 1997.

87
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:36,000
He simply doesn’t discriminate when it comes to the newness of tools.

88
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,600
If it works, it works.

89
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:43,700
Farmers have a long history of invention and is no different today.

90
00:09:43,700 --> 00:09:55,060
Young farmers are guided by their love for agriculture and aided by their knowledge of technology to find inexpensive and appropriately sized tools.

91
00:09:55,060 --> 00:09:58,520
They collaborate and innovate.

92
00:09:58,520 --> 00:10:11,820
Sometimes the old stuff just works better or more efficiently.

93
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:30,040
Q9. What do we learn about Leo Sanchez’s farm?

94
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:50,580
Q10. What has motivated Leo Sanchez and his fellow young farmers to engage in farming?

95
00:10:50,580 --> 00:11:09,440
Q11. Why did Leo Sanchez buy a hand operated weeding tool?

96
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:19,780
PASSAGE 2
Eat Grub is Britain’s first new food company that breaks western food boundaries by introducing edible insects as a new source of food.

97
00:11:19,780 --> 00:11:28,480
And Sainz Breeze is the first UK supermarket to stock the company’s crunchy roasted crickets.

98
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:36,320
Sainz Breeze insist that such food is no joke and could be a new, sustainable source of protein.

99
00:11:36,320 --> 00:11:46,400
Out of curiosity, I paid a visit to Sainz Breeze as I put my hand into a packet of crickets with their tiny eyes and legs.

100
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:51,100
The idea of one going in my mouth made me feel a little sick.

101
00:11:51,100 --> 00:12:02,840
But the first bite was a pleasant surprise, a little dry and lacking of taste, but at least a wing didn’t get stuck in my throat.

102
00:12:02,840 --> 00:12:11,600
The roasted seasoning largely overpowered any other flavour, although there was slightly bitter after taste.

103
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:16,280
The texture is crunchy, but smelt a little of cat food.

104
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:23,380
Eat Grub also recommends the crickets as a topping for noodles, soups and salads.

105
00:12:23,380 --> 00:12:33,920
The company boasts that its dried crickets contain more protein than beef, chicken, and pork, as well as minerals like iron and calcium.

106
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:41,680
Unlike the production of meat, bugs do not use up large amounts of land, water or feed.

107
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:48,180
And insect farming also produces far fewer greenhouse gases.

108
00:12:48,180 --> 00:13:00,380
However, despite 2 billion people worldwide already supplementing their diet with insects, consumer disgust remains a large barrier in many western countries.

109
00:13:00,380 --> 00:13:15,920
I’m not sure bugs will become a popular snack anytime soon, but they’re definitely food for fort.

110
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:35,500
Q12. What do we learn from the passage about the food company Eat Grub?

111
00:13:35,500 --> 00:13:55,060
Q13. What does the speaker say about his first bite a roasted crickets?

112
00:13:55,060 --> 00:14:13,520
Q14. What does Eat Grub say about his dried crickets?

113
00:14:13,520 --> 00:14:59,800
Q15. What does the passage say about insect farming?

114
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:06,260
RECORDING 1
Have you ever had someone try to explain something to you a dozen times with no luck?

115
00:15:06,260 --> 00:15:12,160
But then when you see a picture, the idea finally clicks.

116
00:15:12,160 --> 00:15:18,180
If that sounds familiar, maybe you might consider yourself a visual learner.

117
00:15:18,180 --> 00:15:24,900
Or if reading or listening does a trick, maybe you feel like you’re a verbal learner.

118
00:15:24,900 --> 00:15:28,040
We call these labels learning styles.

119
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:33,520
But is there really a way to categorize different types of students?

120
00:15:33,520 --> 00:15:42,920
Well, it actually seems that multiple presentation formats, especially if one of them is visual, help most people learn.

121
00:15:42,920 --> 00:15:58,240
When psychologists and educators test for learning styles, they’re trying to figure out whether these are inherent traits that affect how well students learn instead of just a preference.

122
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:06,920
Usually they start by giving a survey to figure out what style a student favors, like visual or verbal learning.

123
00:16:06,920 --> 00:16:20,760
Then they try to teach the students something with a specific presentation style, like using visual aids, and do a follow up test to see how much they learned.

124
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:31,980
That way, the researchers can see if the self identified verbal learners really learned better when the information was just spoken aloud, for example.

125
00:16:31,980 --> 00:16:44,100
But according to a 2008 review, only one study that followed this design found that students actually learned best with their preferred style.

126
00:16:44,100 --> 00:16:46,900
But the study had some big flaws.

127
00:16:46,900 --> 00:16:56,300
The researchers excluded 2/3 of the original participants, because they didn’t seem to have any clear learning style from the survey at the beginning.

128
00:16:56,300 --> 00:17:02,240
And they didn’t even report the actual test scores in the final paper.

129
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:09,620
So it doesn’t really seem like learning styles are an inherited trait that we all have.

130
00:17:09,620 --> 00:17:17,980
But that doesn’t mean that all students will do amazingly, if they just spend all their time reading from a textbook.

131
00:17:17,980 --> 00:17:26,760
Instead, most people seem to learn better if they’re taught in several ways, especially if one is visual.

132
00:17:26,760 --> 00:17:36,800
In one study, researchers tested whether students remembered lists of words better if they heard them, saw them or both.

133
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:41,820
And everyone seemed to do better if they got to see the words in print.

134
00:17:41,820 --> 00:17:47,480
Even the self identified auditory learners, their preference didn’t seem to matter.

135
00:17:47,480 --> 00:18:09,180
Similar studies tested whether students learned basic physics and chemistry concepts better by reading plain text or viewing pictures to and everyone do better with the help of pictures.

136
00:18:09,180 --> 00:18:28,800
Q16. Why do psychologists and educators study learning styles?

137
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:49,220
Q17. What does the speaker say about one study mentioned in the 2008 review?

138
00:18:49,220 --> 00:19:09,700
Q18. What message does the speaker want to convey about learning at the end of the talk?

139
00:19:09,700 --> 00:19:13,080
RECORDING 2
Free market capitalism hasn’t freed us.

140
00:19:13,080 --> 00:19:14,860
It has trapped us.

141
00:19:14,860 --> 00:19:18,800
It’s imperative for us to embrace a workplace revolution.

142
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:26,280
We’re unlikely to spend our last moments regretting that we didn’t spend enough of our lives slaving away at work.

143
00:19:26,280 --> 00:19:39,580
We may instead find ourselves feeling guilty about the time we didn’t spend watching our children grow all with our loved ones, or travelling or on the cultural or leisure suits that bring us happiness.

144
00:19:39,580 --> 00:19:46,020
Unfortunately, the average full time employee in the world works 42 hours a week.

145
00:19:46,020 --> 00:19:48,940
Over a 3rd of the time we’re awake.

146
00:19:48,940 --> 00:19:52,800
Some of our all too precious time is being stolen.

147
00:19:52,800 --> 00:19:57,560
Office workers do around 2 billion hours of unpaid overtime each year.

148
00:19:57,560 --> 00:20:06,040
So it’s extremely welcome that some government coalitions have started looking into potentially cutting the working week to four days.

149
00:20:06,040 --> 00:20:13,860
The champions of free market capitalism promised their way of life would bring us freedom, but it wasn’t freedom at all.

150
00:20:13,860 --> 00:20:22,520
From the lack of secure, affordable housing to growing job insecurity and rising personal debt, the individual is trapped.

151
00:20:22,520 --> 00:20:34,020
Nine decades ago, leading economists predicted that technological advances and rising productivity would mean that would be working a 15-hour week by now.

152
00:20:34,020 --> 00:20:37,580
That target has been somewhat missed.

153
00:20:37,580 --> 00:20:56,140
Here is the most malignant threat to our personal freedom, particularly as the balance of power in the workplace has been shifted so dramatically from worker to boss: a huge portion of our lives involves the surrender of our freedom and personal autonomy.

154
00:20:56,140 --> 00:21:04,300
It’s time in which we are directed by the needs and desires of others, and denied the right to make our own choices.

155
00:21:04,300 --> 00:21:05,120
That’s bad for us.

156
00:21:05,120 --> 00:21:13,600
Its hardly surprising that over half a million workers suffer from work-related mental health conditions each year.

157
00:21:13,600 --> 00:21:14,500
All that 15.

158
00:21:14,500 --> 00:21:22,960
4 million working days were lost to work-related stress last year—a jump of nearly a quarter.

159
00:21:22,960 --> 00:21:37,200
Yes, there are those who, far from being overworked, actually seek more hours, but a shorter working week would enable us to redistribute hours from the overworked to the underworked.

160
00:21:37,200 --> 00:21:43,080
We need to look at ways of cutting the working week without slashing living standards.

161
00:21:43,080 --> 00:21:50,460
After all, the world’s workers have already suffered the worst deduction in wages since the early 18 hundreds.

162
00:21:50,460 --> 00:22:10,336
And cutting the working week would be conducive to the individual, giving millions of workers more time to spend as they see fit.

163
00:22:10,336 --> 00:22:27,620
Q19. What do people often feel guilty about according to the speaker?

164
00:22:27,620 --> 00:22:46,600
Q20. What did leading economists predict 90 years ago?

165
00:22:46,600 --> 00:23:02,800
Q21. What is the result of denying workers’ right to make their own choices?

166
00:23:02,800 --> 00:23:10,900
RECORDING 3
Today I'm going to talk about Germany's dream airport in Berlin.

167
00:23:10,900 --> 00:23:20,220
The airport looks exactly like every other major modern airport in Europe, except for one big problem.

168
00:23:20,220 --> 00:23:27,120
More than seven years after it was originally supposed to open, it still stands empty.

169
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:40,460
Germany is known for its efficiency and refined engineering, but when it comes to its new ghost airport, this reputation could not be further from the truth.

170
00:23:40,460 --> 00:23:58,400
Plagued by long delays, perpetual mismanagement, and ever soaring costs, the airport has become something of a joke among Germans and a source of frustration for local politicians, business leaders and residents alike.

171
00:23:58,400 --> 00:24:02,660
Planning for the new airport began in 1989.

172
00:24:02,660 --> 00:24:14,640
At the time, it became clear that the newly reunified Berlin would need a modern airport with far greater capacity than its existing airports.

173
00:24:14,640 --> 00:24:19,720
The city broke ground on the new airport in 2006.

174
00:24:19,720 --> 00:24:31,840
The first major sign of problems came in summer 2010, when the construction corporation pushed the opening from October 2011 to June 2012.

175
00:24:31,840 --> 00:24:36,840
In 2012, the city planned an opening ceremony.

176
00:24:36,840 --> 00:24:49,580
But less than a month before hand, inspectors found significant problems with the fire safety system and push the opening back again to 2013.

177
00:24:49,580 --> 00:24:55,500
It wasn't just the smoke system, many other major problems subsequently emerged.

178
00:24:55,500 --> 00:25:01,820
More than 90 meters of cable were incorrectly installed.

179
00:25:01,820 --> 00:25:05,080
4000 doors were wrongly numbered.

180
00:25:05,080 --> 00:25:07,060
Escalators were too short.

181
00:25:07,060 --> 00:25:10,920
And there was a shortage of check in desks.

182
00:25:10,920 --> 00:25:14,080
So why were so many problems discovered?

183
00:25:14,080 --> 00:25:19,660
Didn't the airport corporation decide to give up on the project and start over?

184
00:25:19,660 --> 00:25:20,820
The reason is simple.

185
00:25:20,820 --> 00:25:33,500
People are often hesitant to terminate a project when they've already invested time or resources into it, even if it might make logical sense to do so.

186
00:25:33,500 --> 00:25:39,080
The longer the delays continued, the more problems inspectors found.

187
00:25:39,080 --> 00:25:46,760
Leadership of the planning corporation has changed hands nearly as many times as the opening date has been pushed back.

188
00:25:46,760 --> 00:25:56,560
Initially, rather than appointing a general contractor to run the project, the corporation decided to manage it themselves.

189
00:25:56,560 --> 00:26:01,620
Despite lack of experience with an undertaking of that scale.

190
00:26:01,620 --> 00:26:08,320
To compound the delays, the unused airport is resulting in massive costs.

191
00:26:08,320 --> 00:26:14,160
Every month it remains unopened costs between nine and 10 million euros.

192
00:26:14,160 --> 00:26:42,880
Assuming all goes well, the airport should open in October 2020, but the still empty airport stands as the biggest embarrassment to Germany's reputation for efficiency and a continuing drain on city and state resources.

193
00:26:42,880 --> 00:27:02,180
Q22. What does the speaker say about the dream airport in Berlin?

194
00:27:02,180 --> 00:27:20,300
Q23. Why was there a need for a new airport in Berlin?

195
00:27:20,300 --> 00:27:40,160
Q24. Why did Berlin postpone the opening of its dream airport again and again?

196
00:27:40,160 --> 00:28:01,006
Q25. What happens while the airport remains unused?
